    "posts": [
      {
        "id": "the-last-hour-on-a-cantilever",
        "slug": "the-last-hour-on-a-cantilever",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "The last hour of light on a coastal cantilever",
          "date_label": "14 September 2026",
          "topic": "Field notes",
          "summary": "Why the best frame of a building often arrives after the sun has gone.",
          "position": 10,
          "photo": "/images/hero-home.webp",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k19",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Most architecture photographs are made in the hour either side of sunset, but the frame people remember usually comes a little later, when the sky has gone soft and the interior lights start to hold their own.",
                  "_key": "k19s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k20",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Plan around the sky",
                  "_key": "k20s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k21",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "We scout every site at least a day early and mark where the sun will fall on each elevation. A cantilever like the one in these pictures only lines up for about twelve minutes.",
                  "_key": "k21s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k22",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Then be patient",
                  "_key": "k22s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k23",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The rest is waiting. We set up the tripod, level it, and shoot in small steps as the light changes. The final image is rarely the one we planned.",
                  "_key": "k23s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "photographing-glass",
        "slug": "photographing-glass",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "Photographing glass: how to make a building disappear",
          "date_label": "2 August 2026",
          "topic": "Technique",
          "summary": "Reflections, balance and the thirty-minute window when inside and outside match.",
          "position": 20,
          "photo": "/images/glass-living.webp",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k24",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Glass is the hardest material to photograph honestly. Too much reflection and the building becomes a mirror; too little and it looks like nothing is there.",
                  "_key": "k24s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k25",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Balance the exposure",
                  "_key": "k25s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k26",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "At dusk the sky and the lit interior are close in brightness. That is when a glazed wall finally reads as a wall, and the view through it feels real.",
                  "_key": "k26s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k27",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A polarising filter helps, but the real tool is timing.",
                  "_key": "k27s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "a-small-stool-and-a-tilt-shift",
        "slug": "a-small-stool-and-a-tilt-shift",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "Why we carry a tilt-shift lens and a small stool",
          "date_label": "19 June 2026",
          "topic": "Kit",
          "summary": "Two inexpensive things that keep verticals straight and viewpoints honest.",
          "position": 30,
          "photo": "/images/camera-lens.webp",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k28",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A tilt-shift lens keeps verticals vertical without distorting a building. A small stool lets us lift the camera by half a metre and see the room the way a visitor does.",
                  "_key": "k28s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k29",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Neither is expensive. Both change the way a space looks.",
                  "_key": "k29s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "briefing-an-interiors-shoot",
        "slug": "briefing-an-interiors-shoot",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "A short brief for photographing interiors",
          "date_label": "21 May 2026",
          "topic": "For architects",
          "summary": "What to prepare before we arrive so the room looks like the architecture you designed.",
          "position": 40,
          "photo": "/images/print-interior.webp",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k30",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The best interior photographs begin with a clear room. Clear surfaces, working lights and curtains that hang straight make an enormous difference.",
                  "_key": "k30s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k31",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Three things to prepare",
                  "_key": "k31s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "k32",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Tell us which views matter most to the architect, when the room is at its best, and who needs to be out of the way. We will plan the rest.",
                  "_key": "k32s"
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            }
          ]
        }
      }
